infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Data vs non-data descriptor: which side of the instance dict
  desc One rule — does it define __set__/__delete__ — decides who wins obj.x
  compares
    - label Data descriptor
      children
        - label Defines __set__ or __delete__
          desc Either method is enough to qualify as a data descriptor
        - label Wins before the instance __dict__ (step 2)
          desc Its __get__ answers first; the instance dict is never consulted
        - label Cannot be shadowed per-instance
          desc self.x = 1 routes through __set__; a smuggled __dict__['x'] stays unreachable
        - label property, validated fields, __slots__
          desc The exact machinery under ORM columns and pydantic models
    - label Non-data descriptor
      children
        - label Defines only __get__
          desc No __set__ and no __delete__ on the class
        - label Loses to the instance __dict__ (step 4)
          desc Checked after the instance dict, like a plain class attribute
        - label Shadowable per-instance
          desc obj.method = lambda: ... overrides it on that one instance
        - label Functions become bound methods
          desc Step 4 calls function.__get__(obj, cls) to make the bound method
theme
  palette #6366f1 #f59e0b
